Functions, tables, domains, and inverse questions (@ql-functions: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @quantitative-literacy, #source @ql-source-algebra, #quantitative-literacy "Graphs-and-interpolation.lingua#r_2GDCZSQKNW5BCGN7B5D9G36HK2") { r_0F4KMKK6ET8T22RG617S1RDZS2
**Read:** Algebra and Trigonometry 2e chapters 3–4 completely. Include domain and range, transformations, composition, inverses, and linear models. Use the chapter reviews to move between a verbal description, table, equation, and graph.

A function specifies one output for each input within a stated domain. The model y = ax + b contains a slope and offset; a nonzero offset means the model is not proportional. Its domain may be restricted by the experiment or by physics. A fitted straight line outside the observed range is an extrapolation, not a new observation.

Suppose a teaching sensor reads 0.2 V at 0 units and 4.2 V at 100 units. A linear conversion is x = 25(V − 0.2 V) in the corresponding numerical-unit convention. State the resulting units explicitly and verify both endpoints. This calculation does not establish linearity between them, temperature stability, resolution, or calibration traceability.

An inverse question asks which input could produce an observed output. A saturating sensor may map several inputs to the same displayed maximum. Then the inverse is not uniquely determined by that reading. A tabulated relation also needs its interpolation rule, conditions, and valid interval.

**Work:** produce tables and graphs for a proportional conversion, an offset conversion, and a capacity-limited process. Write their domains, identify where an inverse exists, and show a misleading extrapolation. Compare a verbal unit statement with the symbols in the formula. Keep the distinction between a definition, a physical law, and an empirical fit visible in the accompanying explanation.

**Continue:** [Read graphs, interpolate, and question a visual claim](Graphs-and-interpolation.lingua#r_2GDCZSQKNW5BCGN7B5D9G36HK2).

